A crossed mixed-effects model carries out that split, with no predictor beyond the intercept,
\begin{equation*}
y = \mu + u_{\text{person}} + u_{\text{painting}} + \varepsilon ,
\end{equation*}
with two independent random intercepts, $u_{\text{person}} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2_{\text{person}})$ and $u_{\text{painting}} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2_{\text{painting}})$, and residual $\varepsilon \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2_{\text{resid}})$. Here $y$ is the rating at one attended second, $\mu$ the typical rating across the cohort, $u_{\text{person}}$ that visitor's offset, $u_{\text{painting}}$ that painting's offset, and $\varepsilon$ the remainder the two offsets leave. Each variance in that notation is the spread of one set of offsets: $\sigma^2_{\text{person}}$ is how widely the visitor offsets scatter around the cohort's typical rating, $\sigma^2_{\text{painting}}$ how widely the painting offsets scatter, and $\sigma^2_{\text{resid}}$ how far individual seconds fall from the offsets that apply to them. Those three are the variance components the split returns. The model is fitted by \ac{REML} on the attended-painting rows, so its participant count is that of the localised evaluation rather than of the full stream.

